Deliver the grown ontology, give User the requests, and let draco initialize its own ontology as its first User

Three increments of 2026-10-12, each settled with Dev one point at a time before building,
in one commit because the shared documents hold all three sets of edits. Dev's full run
passed 735 tests / 53 suites on the tree holding the first two (every headline unchanged);
the third — and the rule alignment it forced — awaits the next run, expected 741 / 54.

ONE. How a caller receives the Editor's grown ontology — the initialization client's
first design point. The accepted ontology was actor state nobody outside could read, and
the Actual is the report on one domain. The ontology has NO CODEC (a domain holds a
TypeDictionary of definitions), so it cannot ride a message or the JSON-codable Actual;
and the Editor already reaches its caller the one way draco actors reach anyone, a
consumer (the consumer-of-M form of 2026-09-18, which holds in any target). Dev accepted
"the ontology consumer on the Editor's factory". The factory's starting state is renamed
`initial`; per Latent, AFTER the Actual, guard(problems.isEmpty).ifThenElse(ontology(accepted), ())
delivers the new state on acceptance only — a rejection delivers nothing, so a caller
keeping its last delivery holds the current state. `()` is the first unit literal in the
drake corpus: the parser carries it as a leaf and both renderers write it back as itself.

TWO. Dev asked what my "client" is to their "User", and restated the original view: Editor
and User support CRUD, User defining the request types and Editor the response types, as
the client/server interface of editing. Accepted reading: the client IS a User — draco as
its own first, endogenous user. Create and Update are the Latent (the ontology decides
which, by identity); Read and Delete are later request types; the Actual is the response.
So `Latent` MOVES to draco.dreams.user as the Create/Update request. Then the mutual
dependency, raised at Dev's request: Editor consumes User's request, a user consumes
Editor's Actual — a cycle the ontology can hold but a Haskell module pair cannot. Accepted:
User declares `Response`, the abstract reply (`problems`, `accepted`; elements, no factory),
and Editor's `Actual` DERIVES it — `type Actual from draco dreams user Response`, the
corpus's first cross-package `from`, written with package words like the domain line. The
Editor's consumer is typed `(draco.dreams.user.Response -> Unit)`, so a user consumes
User.Response and never names Editor.Actual; the dependency runs Editor → User only, and
the ontology states it through derivation, the relationship it already projects. The
generator put the inherited `problems` into Actual's codec and factory unprompted (the
Aspects/TypeDefinition precedent) and imported the parent's package.

THREE. The client itself. Dev accepted "the computed dependency order from an empty
ontology". `draco.dreams.Initialization`, the Dreams domain's first member — Dreams is the
constructing layer, and the client must name both User (requests) and Editor (the actor),
which neither of those two may do without re-creating the cycle TWO removed. It takes the
canonical domains as Latents (supplied, not loaded: the consuming project owns persistence;
the test supplies them through DomainBuilder from the resource tree) and ORDERS them from
the definitions: `owns`, `parents` (named parents outside the Latent itself), `ready` (every
parent some supplied domain owns is already placed), and `order`, a recursive dyn that
places a wave at a time, keeps the supplied order within a wave, treats a parent nobody
supplied as no constraint (the Editor refuses it), and appends the remainder unchanged
when no wave forms, so a cycle cannot hang it. Every loaded definition is rooted at
DracoType, so Draco alone is the first wave. `editorType` mints the Editor over
`DomainOntology()` with the client's own consumers, `note` (every Response into a `[Response]+`)
and `keep` (`mut ontology`, the last delivery); `run(editor)` sends the ordered Latents;
`complete` and `accepted` read the state. The first `mut` element in a plain type: the
trait declares `var`, the factory body binds `override var`. Nothing is hand-listed:
adding a domain to the corpus never means editing the client.

FOUND BY THE ENDOGENOUS RUN, and aligned in this commit: the run refused Format, then
XML, JSON and Value dangling behind it. Format(F) is the corpus's one parameterized
domain; its self-declaration — and every member's container pointer — is the bare
`domain draco format Format`, and the SelfDeclaration rule of 2026-10-09 compared the
WHOLE TypeName. Dev's later principle decides it (2026-10-12, Uniqueness): parameters
compose references, not definitions; a self-declaration points at the definition, so it
is keyed by name and package. `SelfDeclaration` now compares `namePath`, as does
DomainBuilder.validate. SelfDeclarationRulesTest's parameter-mismatch test is inverted and
checks Format(F) itself; EditorTest's case now refuses a self-declaration naming ANOTHER
definition and accepts the bare one. This is a consequence of Dev's principle applied by
the model, not a decision Dev took in dialogue — flagged in the handoff for correction.

